<DocsLayout title="MCP Server for Coding Agents" description="Give Claude Code, Cursor and Codex your design tokens so they stop hardcoding values">
  <h1>MCP Server for Coding Agents</h1>

  <p>
    Coding agents hardcode values because they never read your token file. <code>buoy mcp</code>
    gives Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and Windsurf four tools that answer "what tokens does this repo
    have?" and "did I just drift?" while the agent is writing, so the fix happens before the PR.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>

  <p><strong>Claude Code plugin</strong> (MCP server, post-edit hook and a design-tokens skill in one install):</p>

  <pre><code>/plugin marketplace add ahoybuoy/buoy
/plugin install buoy@buoy</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Any MCP client</strong>, or Claude Code without the plugin system:</p>

  <pre><code>npx @buoy-design/cli mcp install claude    # or cursor, codex, windsurf, all</code></pre>

  <p>For Claude Code this writes two files into the repo:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>.mcp.json</code> — registers the <code>buoy</code> MCP server (runs <code>buoy mcp serve</code> over stdio)</li>
    <li><code>.claude/settings.json</code> — a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that checks every style file Claude edits</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Other clients get their own <code>mcp.json</code> (<code>.cursor/</code>, <code>.codex/</code>, <code>.windsurf/</code>).
    Existing servers and hooks in those files are kept. Commit the files so everyone on the team gets the same agent setup.
  </p>

  <h2>Tools</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What the agent gets</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>list_design_tokens</code></td><td>Every token in the repo (CSS custom properties, Tailwind theme, tokens.json, SCSS), filterable by category or name. Meant to be called before writing any style.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>find_token_for_value</code></td><td>The token whose value equals a literal the agent is about to write: <code>#1a73e8</code> → <code>--color-primary</code>. Handles hex shorthand, case and units.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>check_design_drift</code></td><td>Line-level drift in the files just edited, each issue with the token to use instead. Without files, the full repository analysis.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>design_system_context</code></td><td>A Markdown overview of the system: token categories, components, and the anti-patterns Buoy flags. One call at the start of a UI task.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The hook</h2>

  <p>
    After Claude writes or edits a <code>.tsx</code>, <code>.jsx</code>, <code>.vue</code>, <code>.svelte</code>,
    <code>.css</code>, <code>.scss</code> or <code>.html</code> file, <code>buoy mcp hook</code> checks that file and,
    if it drifted, returns feedback Claude acts on immediately:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Buoy: 2 design drift issues in src/components/Hero.css
- src/components/Hero.css:2 Hardcoded color #1a2744 has a token: --color-navy -> use var(--color-navy)
- src/components/Hero.css:3 Hardcoded spacing 8px (no matching token)
Replace the literal values with the tokens above, then continue.</code></pre>

  <p>
    Files that are already tokenised produce no output. A hook failure never blocks the agent; it exits 0 with a note.
  </p>

  <h2>Without an install step</h2>

  <p>Buoy is listed in the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0/servers?search=io.github.ahoybuoy/buoy">official MCP Registry</a> as <code>io.github.ahoybuoy/buoy</code>. Any MCP client can also register the server by hand:</p>

  <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "buoy": &#123; "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@buoy-design/cli", "mcp", "serve"] &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>And any agent runner can call the hook directly: <code>buoy mcp hook --file src/Button.tsx</code> (exit code 2 means drift, with details on stderr).</p>

  <h2>Pairs with the GitHub App</h2>

  <p>
    The MCP server catches drift while the agent writes; the <a href="/docs/integrations/github-actions">GitHub App</a>
    catches what slipped through on the PR, from agents and people alike, and a <a href="/docs/features/ai-guardrails">DESIGN.md</a> gives both the same rules.
    Repos with no tokens yet can run <code>buoy dock tokens</code> to extract a set from the values already in use.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/marketplace/buoy-design"><strong>Install the GitHub App</strong></a>. It is free, and installing it is
    all it takes: the first pull request is reviewed with no Buoy account or sign-up. Sign in to the dashboard later if you want
    history and trends; your reviews come with you.
  </p>

